---
// Docs for the public data: what each JSON file holds and how to use it.
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
import { REPO_URL, apis, leaderboard, meta } from "../../lib/data";
import { SITE } from "../../lib/feed";

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, "");
const example = `curl -s ${SITE}/data/index.json | jq '.apis[] | {name, score}'`;
// The first entry of the real index, so the example always matches what the endpoint returns.
const top = leaderboard[0];
const sample = JSON.stringify(
  {
    version: 1,
    generatedAt: meta.generatedAt,
    apis: [
      {
        id: top.id, name: top.name, rank: top.rank, score: top.score.value, last12: top.last12, endpoints: top.operations,
        page: `${SITE}/api/${top.id}/`, data: `${SITE}/data/${top.id}.json`, feed: `${SITE}/feed/${top.id}.xml`, badge: `${SITE}/badge/${top.id}.svg`,
      },
      "…",
    ],
  },
  null,
  2,
);
---

<Base title="Data: API Weather" description="Every number on API Weather as free JSON: scores, monthly counts and every breaking change, updated daily.">
  <article class="prose">
    <h1>Data</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      Every number on this site is free to use as JSON. The files are static, served from GitHub Pages, and rebuilt every day
      after the pipeline runs. No key, no rate limit beyond GitHub's, and CORS is open.
    </p>

    <h2>Endpoints</h2>
    <dl>
      <dt><a href={`${base}/data/index.json`}><code>/data/index.json</code></a></dt>
      <dd>All {apis.length} APIs, ranked, with each one's score, last-12-month totals and links to everything below.</dd>
      <dt><code>/data/&#123;id&#125;.json</code>, e.g. <a href={`${base}/data/stripe.json`}><code>/data/stripe.json</code></a></dt>
      <dd>
        One API's full record: every monthly snapshot, every interval with its breaking and soft changes, per-year totals, and
        the score. Changes list up to five example endpoints in <code>operations</code> and every endpoint they touched in
        <code>opIds</code>, which index into the file's <code>endpoints</code> list.
      </dd>
      <dt><code>/feed/&#123;id&#125;.xml</code> and <a href={`${base}/feed.xml`}><code>/feed.xml</code></a></dt>
      <dd>RSS: one item per month that broke something, for one API or all of them.</dd>
      <dt><code>/badge/&#123;id&#125;.svg</code></dt>
      <dd>A README badge with the current score.</dd>
    </dl>

    <h2>Example</h2>
    <pre><code>{example}</code></pre>
    <pre><code>{sample}</code></pre>

    <h2>Fields worth knowing</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><code>hard</code> is breaking changes, after grouping one schema change across all the endpoints it touches.</li>
      <li><code>soft</code> is additive changes that can still trip strict clients, such as a new enum value.</li>
      <li><code>hardUnstable</code> is breaking changes that only touched endpoints the vendor marks beta; they're not in <code>hard</code> or the score.</li>
      <li><code>gap</code> marks months with no readable spec, and <code>error</code> months the diff couldn't complete. Neither counts as zero.</li>
      <li><code>partial</code> marks the current month, which changes daily until it ends.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Stability</h2>
    <p>
      Fields are only added, never renamed or removed, while <code>version</code> is 1. The same files are in the repo under
      <a href={`${REPO_URL}/tree/main/data`}><code>data/</code></a>, with a write-up of the format in its README. If you build
      something with it, a link back is appreciated.
    </p>
  </article>
</Base>

<style>
  .prose {
    padding-top: clamp(2rem, 6vw, 4rem);
    max-width: 44rem;
    display: grid;
    gap: 1rem;
  }

  h1 {
    font-size: clamp(2.5rem, 7vw, var(--t5));
    letter-spacing: -0.04em;
    margin-bottom: 0.5rem;
  }

  .lede {
    font-size: var(--t1);
    color: var(--ink-2);
  }

  h2 {
    font-size: var(--t2);
    margin-top: 1.75rem;
  }

  dl {
    margin: 0;
    display: grid;
    gap: 0.35rem;
  }

  dt {
    margin-top: 0.75rem;
    font-weight: 600;
  }

  dd {
    margin: 0;
    color: var(--ink-2);
  }

  ul {
    margin: 0;
    padding-left: 1.25rem;
    display: grid;
    gap: 0.5rem;
  }

  pre {
    margin: 0;
    padding: 1rem 1.25rem;
    background: var(--wash);
    border-radius: 6px;
    overflow-x: auto;
    line-height: 1.6;
    font-size: 0.8125rem;
  }

  pre code {
    background: none;
    padding: 0;
  }
</style>
